WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.236
-Our next guest is
a talented writer and actress

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:04.938
who stars in "Rutherford Falls"
on Peacock.

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.572
All episodes of
the second season premiere

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:07.807
Thursday, June 16th.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.208
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:11.343
-I'm not drinking all this.
This is like a kiddie pool.

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:13.480
-There's all kinds of life hacks
to help you optimize --

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.083
cold showers, random sprints,
calibrating chews per bite.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.250
I'll send you some articles.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.718
-The last thing I need is

00:00:19.718 --> 00:00:21.721
homework telling me
how to do more homework.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:23.356
I need a real support staff.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.024
I'm doing the work of
three people.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:26.325
-The revamp drained us.

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:28.561
We should be cutting budgets,
not expanding them.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:31.330
But I might have something
that could solve your problems.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.665
-Is it the water?

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:33.766
-The water, yes.

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:35.167
-Mm-hmm.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.071
-Please welcome to the show
Jana Schmieding, everybody!

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:40.140
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.487
Hi, Jana!
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:56.556
-Oh, my gosh.
I am so happy to be here.

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.591
This is wild.
-This is very exciting.

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.226
What do you have?
-I have a gift for you.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.228
-Oh, my God. That's so nice.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.930
-I consulted your friend
and my E.P. of

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.398
"Rutherford Falls," Mike Schur.

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:08.635
-Mike Schur, who created
this show and many others.

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:10.203
-Yes, many others.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.904
And he said you were
a Boston Red Sox fan,

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.205
so I beaded you --

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:16.909
-You beaded me a hat!
Look at that! It's gorgeous!

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:20.113
Are you -- Now, is beading --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:23.649
Is beading something
that you often do?

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:25.551
And did you take to it
right away?

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:27.553
It looks gorgeous.
-Ugh, it looks so dang good.

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:30.622
-So dang good. Look at that.
-God!

00:01:30.622 --> 00:01:32.525
Yes, I am an avid beader.

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.260
I have been beading
since I was 8 years old.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.230
It is a traditional art form
that the lot of native people

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.199
do, and I am --

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:41.534
-Were you good at it right away
as an 8-year-old?

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:42.735
-No!
-Okay.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:45.204
-I don't even think
I'm that good at it now.

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:47.874
-Oh.
Well, then I don't want this.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.609
-Yeah. It's trash.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:53.145
-So, "Rutherford Falls"
is a show that has

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:55.681
not only native representation
in the writer's room,

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:56.983
but on camera.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.452
And I think
what's unique about it is,

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:02.821
it represents native people
in a modern setting,

00:02:02.821 --> 00:02:04.424
because I feel like,
more often than not,

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:06.825
when we see native people
on screen, it's about the past.

00:02:06.825 --> 00:02:12.231
-Yes, we, as a people, are often
trapped by the industry

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:18.504
in the past and are depicted,
through sort of a white lens,

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:19.939
as existing in the past.

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:23.276
And it's actually -- Like, the
statistics are pretty staggering

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:27.380
about what America
knows about native people,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.315
about indigenous populations.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:32.218
I think it's something like
80% of people

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:36.623
don't know that
native people still exist.

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.859
That's the -- Yeah, it's wild.
It's bad.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:43.195
-What happens --
Do you find that there is

00:02:43.195 --> 00:02:44.997
sort of a uniform reaction

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:47.533
when you tell people
that you are native?

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:52.305
-Yeah, well, a lot of people
like to say, "Me too.

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:53.806
So am I."

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:54.974
-I'm sorry.
And this is white people?

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:56.008
-Yeah.
-Oh, no.

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:59.979
-Uh-huh. Uh-huh.
A lot of white people.

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.748
I'm not going to
take a poll in here,

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:05.250
but I bet half of you
believe you're Cherokee.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.586
-Is Cherokee the go-to
for white people who --

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:07.720
-It is.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:13.125
I don't -- I feel so bad
for the Cherokee Nation.

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:15.727
They have a lot of, you know,

00:03:15.727 --> 00:03:19.933
sort of self-identifying members
of their tribal nation.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:22.701
-The other amazing thing is,

00:03:22.701 --> 00:03:24.804
you were hired as a writer
on this show first, yes?

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:27.239
And then you were cast as
the lead in this show.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.975
And the co-lead with Ed Helms,
I should say.

00:03:29.975 --> 00:03:33.313
And, also, I read that
this was going to be it for you.

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:36.248
You were about to hang it up
and leave L.A.

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:37.450
-Oh, yeah.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:42.554
I moved to L.A. from New York
in the attempt to

00:03:42.554 --> 00:03:43.890
write for television,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.626
and all of my samples,
my writing samples,

00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:51.730
centered around
a native woman protagonist,

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.299
and it wasn't getting anywhere.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:55.968
I mean, I was applying for
all of the fellowships,

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:58.237
sending my script to
Austin Film Festival,

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:01.274
being like,
"Oh, this is getting in, baby!"

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:06.245
I was so confident
and didn't get a single bite.

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:10.883
And so by 2019, late 2019,
I was really frustrated.

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.885
I was starting to,
like, internalize the messaging

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.621
that I was getting that either
I'm not a good writer

00:04:15.621 --> 00:04:19.758
or people aren't interested in
native women in comedy.

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:23.462
And I was like, "If I don't get
staffed, you know,

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:27.233
in early 2020,
I'm throwing in the towel, man!

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:28.734
I'm packing it up."

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.170
And I was planning to just move
back to Oregon with my parents

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.905
and -- I don't know --
work at a bar?

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:34.106
-Wow!

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.775
Well, this worked out fantastic.
-It really did.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.977
-It should be noted that
on your way,

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.912
like a lot of people
on your journey

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.547
to try to make it
in this business,

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.649
you were in New York
for a while and you were

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:46.819
trying to make it in comedy
and also teaching in the Bronx.

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:48.354
What kind of teacher were you?

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:52.925
-I was a humanities teacher in
middle school and high school.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.427
-Alright.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:56.829
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.565
-Did your students
know their teacher

00:04:59.565 --> 00:05:02.068
was trying to have a career
in comedy at the time?

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:03.403
-Well, yes.

00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.170
I kind of kept it private
for the most part

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.606
while I was a teacher,

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:09.208
but in the last year, I was
sort of like, "Whatever, man.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.844
I'm trying to be a star, guys."

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.913
-Well, I'm very glad
that it worked out

00:05:14.913 --> 00:05:16.715
and that maybe you weren't
the best teacher,

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:18.417
but I think they will see you
as a message...

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:19.651
-It's so true.

00:05:19.651 --> 00:05:21.353
-...that anybody can be
what they dream to be.

00:05:21.353 --> 00:05:24.123
-That's right.
I wasn't the best teacher.

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:26.626
-Well, obviously,
you have found the path

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:27.727
that is best suited to you.

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:28.961
And thank you so much
for being here.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:30.029
It's such a delight.
And thank you for the hat!

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:31.697
-Thank you for having me!
Thank you!

00:05:31.697 --> 00:05:33.733
-You guys,
that's Jana Schmieding.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:37.169
All episodes of the second
season of "Rutherford Falls"

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:39.905
with her and Ed Helms
premiere Thursday, June 16th.

00:05:39.905 --> 00:05:41.074
We'll be right back.

